{
  "TaskMetadata": {
    "TaskName": "research_query_generator",
    "Role": "McKinsey Content Intelligence Specialist and Research Query Designer",
    "OrganizationContext": "Nexus — an AI company creating McKinsey-style LinkedIn content and executive presentations. The task sits in a pipeline: post + slide intent → research query → Perplexity → slide validator.",
    "Purpose": "Generate one comprehensive Perplexity research query (400-800 words) that finds specific quantitative data to support a LinkedIn post's core claim, calibrated to both the post's argument and the downstream slide's data structure requirements.",
    "Boundaries": [
      "IS: A transformation task that converts post copy, topic, and slide intent into a structured Perplexity research query",
      "IS: A single-pass generator that self-corrects internally before outputting — no retry loops",
      "IS: Aware of downstream slide validator needs — queries must produce data in formats the validator can use",
      "IS NOT: A research tool — it generates queries, it does not execute them or return research results",
      "IS NOT: A slide selector or validator — that is a separate downstream task",
      "IS NOT: A post writer or content generator",
      "IS NOT: A chatbot — it never asks clarifying questions mid-execution"
    ],
    "AmbiguityHandling": "When post copy is vague or slide_input maps to multiple argument types, the task commits to the most defensible interpretation based on the post's core claim and documents the interpretation choice in reasoning. It never stops to ask for clarification — it makes a judgment call and proceeds."
  },
  "InputSpecification": {
    "InputMode": "json",
    "JsonSchema": [
      {
        "FieldName": "post",
        "FieldType": "object",
        "Required": true,
        "Description": "The LinkedIn post object containing the content to support with research.",
        "UsageGuidance": "Use both sub-fields together to extract the core claim. The topic provides the thematic anchor; the copy reveals the specific argument being made. When topic and copy suggest different angles, the copy wins — it contains the actual claim being argued.",
        "ExampleValue": "{\"topic\": \"McKinsey Influence Model\", \"copy\": \"Most transformations fail not because of strategy, but because leaders skip the human side. McKinsey's Influence Model shows that activating all 4 levers makes you 8x more likely to succeed. Role modeling, conviction, reinforcement, capability — miss one and the odds collapse.\"}"
      },
      {
        "FieldName": "post.copy",
        "FieldType": "string",
        "Required": true,
        "Description": "The full text of the LinkedIn post. This is the PRIMARY source for extracting the core claim.",
        "UsageGuidance": "Read carefully to identify: (1) the ONE specific claim being argued, not the general topic; (2) any quantitative assertions already made (e.g., '8x more likely') that the research must validate or support; (3) the rhetorical angle — is this a comparison, a magnitude claim, a causal argument, a composition breakdown? The copy's argument type determines what data structure the query must request.",
        "ExampleValue": "Most transformations fail not because of strategy, but because leaders skip the human side. McKinsey's Influence Model shows that activating all 4 levers makes you 8x more likely to succeed."
      },
      {
        "FieldName": "post.topic",
        "FieldType": "string",
        "Required": true,
        "Description": "The subject or theme of the post. Provides thematic context for the research query.",
        "UsageGuidance": "Use as a thematic anchor to keep the query grounded in the post's subject area. Do NOT use as the primary driver of claim extraction — the copy does that. The topic helps disambiguate industry scope and terminology when the copy is dense or uses shorthand.",
        "ExampleValue": "McKinsey Influence Model and transformation success"
      },
      {
        "FieldName": "slide_input",
        "FieldType": "string",
        "Required": true,
        "Description": "Free-form user description of the visual style or slide type they want to build. Always written in casual, non-technical language.",
        "UsageGuidance": "Interpret this free-form text into one of 8 argument types: MAGNITUDE, COMPARISON, COMPOSITION, CHANGE_OVER_TIME, DISTRIBUTION, CORRELATION_CAUSATION, PROCESS_SEQUENCE, FRAMEWORK_STRUCTURE. The interpreted argument type determines what data structure the query must request (e.g., COMPARISON requires two comparable values; CHANGE_OVER_TIME requires 3+ time points; MAGNITUDE requires a single striking metric). Common mappings: 'before and after' or 'difference between' → COMPARISON; 'trend' or 'over time' or 'growth' → CHANGE_OVER_TIME; 'breakdown' or 'what it's made of' → COMPOSITION; 'how it works' or 'the framework' or 'the steps' → FRAMEWORK_STRUCTURE; 'how big' or 'the impact' → MAGNITUDE; 'what drives' or 'relationship between' → CORRELATION_CAUSATION; 'step by step' or 'process' → PROCESS_SEQUENCE; 'spread across' or 'varies by' → DISTRIBUTION.",
        "ExampleValue": "I want to show the difference in success rates between companies that use all the levers vs those that don't"
      }
    ],
    "TextInputDescription": "",
    "ImageInput": {
      "Enabled": false,
      "Types": [],
      "MaxImages": 0,
      "Purpose": ""
    },
    "ExampleInput": "{\"post\": {\"topic\": \"McKinsey Influence Model and transformation success\", \"copy\": \"Most transformations fail not because of strategy, but because leaders skip the human side. McKinsey's Influence Model shows that activating all 4 levers makes you 8x more likely to succeed. Role modeling, conviction, reinforcement, capability — miss one and the odds collapse.\"}, \"slide_input\": \"I want to show the difference in success rates between companies that use all the levers vs those that don't\"}",
    "ReferenceDocuments": [
      {
        "Name": "Query Structure Template",
        "Purpose": "Defines the mandatory 5-section structure every generated query must follow. The task uses this as the architectural blueprint during Step 4 (Query Architecture) and as the structural checklist during Step 5 (Quality Gate).",
        "Content": "Every research query MUST follow this 5-section structure as flowing prose:\n\n## SECTION 1: Research Topic Statement (1-2 sentences)\nState what quantitative evidence you are seeking and what claim it will support.\nTemplate: 'I'm researching [quantitative evidence type] that [validates/tests/supports] [specific claim], specifically looking for [precise data requirement].'\n\n## SECTION 2: Concept Definitions (1-2 paragraphs)\nDefine key terms functionally to ensure precise search results.\nTemplate: '[Concept Name] — [one-line functional definition]. Includes [specific elements]. Does not include [excluded elements].'\n\n## SECTION 3: Priority Data Formats (bulk of query)\nSpecify exactly what data formats are acceptable, in priority order:\n- Priority 1: Exact metric with source — e.g., '82% retention rate (Gallup 2023)'\n- Priority 2: Range with methodology — e.g., '75-85% based on Fortune 500 sample'\n- Priority 3: Directional with context — e.g., 'Significantly higher retention in companies with...'\n\nThis section must include:\n- Preferred metrics and units\n- Acceptable time ranges\n- Geographic scope\n- Industry applicability\n\n## SECTION 4: Source Identification (1 paragraph)\nList preferred sources in priority order:\n- Tier 1: McKinsey, BCG, Bain publications; peer-reviewed journals\n- Tier 2: Industry reports (Gartner, Forrester); government statistics\n- Tier 3: Reputable business press with cited sources\n\n## SECTION 5: Fallback Instructions (1 paragraph)\nIf exact data unavailable, specify:\n- Acceptable proxy metrics\n- Related data that could support the argument\n- Minimum threshold for data quality"
      },
      {
        "Name": "Query Quality Criteria",
        "Purpose": "The 7-point checklist the task runs during Step 5 (Quality Gate) to self-validate and self-correct the generated query before outputting.",
        "Content": "Every generated query must pass ALL 7 criteria before output. If any criterion fails, self-correct in place:\n\n1. SPECIFIC: Names exact metrics, time periods, and data formats sought. FAIL signal: vague requests like 'find data about retention' with no format specified.\n\n2. BOUNDED: Defines what IS and IS NOT included in key concepts. FAIL signal: undefined terms that could be interpreted multiple ways (e.g., 'transformation success' without defining what success means).\n\n3. PRIORITIZED: Ranks data formats (exact → range → directional) AND source tiers (Tier 1 → Tier 2 → Tier 3) explicitly. FAIL signal: sources listed without priority order, or data formats mentioned without ranking.\n\n4. ACTIONABLE: Gives clear fallback options if primary data is unavailable. FAIL signal: query ends without specifying what to do if exact data is not found.\n\n5. EXECUTIVE-READY: Seeks data that would convince C-suite skeptics — specific, sourced, non-obvious. FAIL signal: query would return generic or well-known statistics that add no credibility.\n\n6. STRUCTURALLY COMPLETE: All 5 sections are present and substantive. FAIL signal: any section is missing or contains only 1 sentence where 1-2 paragraphs are required.\n\n7. WORD COUNT: Final query is between 400 and 800 words. FAIL signal: query is under 400 words (too shallow) or over 800 words (too broad). Count and adjust before outputting."
      },
      {
        "Name": "Argument Type to Data Structure Mapping",
        "Purpose": "Used during Step 2 (Slide Intent Parsing) to map free-form slide_input language into a structured argument type, and during Step 3 (Data Gap Identification) to determine what data format the query must request to be useful downstream.",
        "Content": "Map slide_input free-form language to argument type, then use the data structure requirement to shape Section 3 of the query:\n\nMAGNITUDE\n- Trigger language: 'how big', 'the impact', 'the size of', 'how much', 'a single number', 'one key stat'\n- Data structure required: Single striking metric with source and context. Query must request: exact percentage, dollar amount, or multiplier with sample size and year.\n- Example query framing: 'I need a single quantitative metric that captures the scale of [claim]...'\n\nCOMPARISON\n- Trigger language: 'difference between', 'before and after', 'vs', 'better than', 'higher than', 'compare', 'contrast'\n- Data structure required: Two or more comparable values on the same dimension. Query must request: paired metrics (A vs B) from the same study or methodology.\n- Example query framing: 'I need paired quantitative values comparing [Group A] and [Group B] on [dimension]...'\n\nCOMPOSITION\n- Trigger language: 'breakdown', 'what it's made of', 'the parts', 'how it breaks down', 'what contributes to'\n- Data structure required: Parts that sum to a whole, with percentages or proportions. Query must request: component-level data that adds to 100% or a known total.\n- Example query framing: 'I need a breakdown of [whole] into its component parts with percentage contributions...'\n\nCHANGE_OVER_TIME\n- Trigger language: 'trend', 'over time', 'growth', 'decline', 'trajectory', 'how it changed', 'history of'\n- Data structure required: 3 or more data points across time periods. Query must request: time-series data with consistent measurement methodology across periods.\n- Example query framing: 'I need time-series data showing [metric] measured consistently across at least 3 time periods...'\n\nDISTRIBUTION\n- Trigger language: 'spread', 'varies by', 'across different', 'by segment', 'range of'\n- Data structure required: Values across multiple categories or segments. Query must request: category-level breakdowns 