    "Examples": [
      {
        "Name": "Clear COMPARISON claim — Influence Model lever activation",
        "Input": "{\"post\": {\"topic\": \"McKinsey Influence Model and transformation success\", \"copy\": \"Most transformations fail not because of strategy, but because leaders skip the human side. McKinsey's Influence Model shows that activating all 4 levers makes you 8x more likely to succeed. Role modeling, conviction, reinforcement, capability — miss one and the odds collapse.\"}, \"slide_input\": \"I want to show the difference in success rates between companies that use all the levers vs those that don't\"}",
        "Reasoning": "Core claim: activating all 4 Influence Model levers yields 8x higher transformation success vs fewer levers. Existing metric in post: 8x multiplier — this means the data gap is the underlying success rate percentages, not the multiplier itself. Slide intent 'show the difference in success rates' maps unambiguously to COMPARISON — requires paired values on the same dimension from the same study. Data gap: absolute success rate percentages for all-4-lever vs fewer-lever organizations. Query architecture: Section 1 anchors to 8x claim and requests underlying percentages. Section 2 defines Influence Model activation (all 4 simultaneous vs subset) and transformation success (met original objectives, not technology-only). Section 3 prioritizes exact paired percentages first, then ranges, then multiplier with stated baseline. Section 4 prioritizes McKinsey primary sources. Section 5 names BCG transformation research and four-building-blocks research as proxies. Quality gate: all 5 sections present, rankings explicit, fallback named, word count 521 — within range.",
        "Output": "{\"reasoning\": \"Core claim: activating all 4 Influence Model levers yields 8x higher transformation success vs fewer levers. Existing metric in post: 8x multiplier — data gap is the underlying success rate percentages. Slide intent maps to COMPARISON. Data structure required: paired values from same study. All 5 sections constructed, quality gate passed, word count 521.\", \"query\": \"I'm researching paired quantitative evidence comparing transformation success rates between organizations that activate all four components of McKinsey's Influence Model versus those that activate fewer components, specifically looking for the underlying success rate percentages that produce the reported 8x multiplier effect and any data on how success rates degrade as fewer levers are activated.\\n\\nMcKinsey's Influence Model refers to the four-lever behavior-change framework comprising: role modeling by senior leaders who visibly demonstrate desired new behaviors; fostering understanding and conviction among employees; reinforcing mechanisms including incentives, processes, and systems that reward new behaviors; and building confidence and skills through capability development. Activating all four levers means an organization has implemented initiatives across all four components simultaneously during a transformation. Does not include organizations addressing only cultural or only structural elements, or programs addressing levers sequentially. Transformation success means a large-scale organizational change program that met or exceeded its originally stated objectives within the intended timeframe as assessed by study authors. Excludes technology-only implementations, cost reduction programs, and incremental process improvements not involving organizational behavior change.\\n\\nFor data formats, I prefer in priority order: first, exact paired success rate percentages from the same study sample showing outcomes for all-four-lever organizations versus fewer-lever organizations, with sample size and study year stated, for example 79% versus 25% based on 500 transformations from McKinsey 2023; second, a range of success rates with methodology described, for example 70 to 85% for full-activation organizations based on a Fortune 500 sample over five years; third, a multiplier with a stated baseline, for example 8x more likely where the single-lever baseline rate is explicitly given. Studies conducted between 2015 and 2024 preferred. Geographic scope global with preference for multi-country samples. Data from studies with fewer than 100 organizations should be flagged as low statistical power.\\n\\nFor sources, prioritize in this order: first, McKinsey and Company primary publications including McKinsey Quarterly, McKinsey Global Institute reports, and McKinsey dot com insights particularly from the People and Organizational Performance practice; second, BCG Henderson Institute transformation research, Bain and Company insights, or peer-reviewed journals including Harvard Business Review research articles and Academy of Management Journal; third, Gartner or Forrester change management research, PROSCI benchmarking reports, or business press articles explicitly citing primary research with stated methodology.\\n\\nIf Influence Model lever-activation data is not publicly available, acceptable proxies include McKinsey research on the four building blocks of change showing success rate differentials by number of actions taken, BCG transformation research segmented by number of change management practices implemented, or any peer-reviewed study comparing holistic versus partial behavior-change interventions with quantified success rate differentials. Minimum threshold: a quantified differential between high-activation and low-activation groups from a named study with at least 50 organizations published after 2010.\"}"
      },
      {
        "Name": "MAGNITUDE claim — no existing metric in post",
        "Input": "{\"post\": {\"topic\": \"Cost of employee turnover\", \"copy\": \"Every time a company loses a skilled employee, the real cost is invisible on the balance sheet. Recruiting, onboarding, lost productivity, team disruption — it adds up to far more than the salary. Most CFOs are underestimating this by an order of magnitude.\"}, \"slide_input\": \"I want one big number that shows how expensive turnover really is\"}",
        "Reasoning": "Core claim: the true cost of employee turnover is significantly underestimated by CFOs — implied to be much larger than salary replacement cost. No existing metric in post — post makes a qualitative assertion about underestimation without stating a number. Data gap: need a striking quantitative metric for total turnover cost as a percentage of salary or absolute dollar figure, from a credible source, that would surprise a CFO. Slide intent 'one big number' maps unambiguously to MAGNITUDE — single metric, no comparison needed. Data structure required: single striking metric with source, context, and year. Query anchored to total cost framing including hidden costs (productivity loss, team disruption, knowledge transfer) not just recruiting costs. Quality gate: all 5 sections present, word count 468, rankings explicit.",
        "Output": "{\"reasoning\": \"Core claim: true turnover cost is severely underestimated — implied to be much larger than salary. No existing metric in post. Slide intent maps to MAGNITUDE — single striking number. Data gap: total turnover cost as percentage of salary or dollar figure including hidden costs. Query built to capture full-cost framing including productivity loss and team disruption. Quality gate passed, word count 468.\", \"query\": \"I'm researching a single authoritative quantitative metric capturing the total cost of employee turnover for skilled workers, specifically looking for a figure that includes both direct costs such as recruiting and onboarding and indirect costs such as productivity loss, knowledge transfer, and team disruption, expressed either as a percentage of annual salary or as an absolute dollar amount per departing employee.\\n\\nEmployee turnover cost refers to the total financial impact on an organization when a skilled employee voluntarily or involuntarily departs, including all costs required to replace that employee and restore full productivity. Includes direct costs such as job posting fees, recruiter time, interviewing costs, background checks, onboarding expenses, and training time. Also includes indirect costs such as productivity loss during the vacancy period, reduced output from the departing employee in their final weeks, knowledge transfer costs, and team disruption effects on remaining employees. Does not include costs associated with layoffs or restructuring where the role is eliminated. Skilled workers refers to employees with specialized knowledge, professional qualifications, or more than two years of role-specific experience — not entry-level or easily replaceable positions.\\n\\nFor data formats, I prefer in priority order: first, an exact total cost figure expressed as a percentage of annual salary with source, year, and methodology stated explicitly, for example 150% of annual salary based on a sample of 500 US companies from SHRM 2022; second, a dollar amount per departing employee with job category and industry specified, for example $45,000 per departing mid-level professional in financial services; third, a range with methodology, for example 50% to 200% of annual salary depending on seniority level. Studies from 2015 to 2024 preferred. US or global scope acceptable. Industry-specific data should be labeled as such.\\n\\nFor sources, prioritize in this order: first, SHRM research, McKinsey talent analytics publications, or peer-reviewed human resources or organizational behavior journals; second, Gallup workplace research, Deloitte human capital reports, or Gartner HR research; third, reputable business press including Harvard Business Review or Wall Street Journal articles that explicitly cite primary research with methodology.\\n\\nIf a total cost figure is unavailable, acceptable proxies include: Gallup data on productivity loss from disengaged employees during transition periods; LinkedIn Talent Solutions data on time-to-fill and cost-per-hire for skilled roles; any study separating hard dollar recruiting costs from soft productivity costs with both components quantified. Minimum threshold: study must quantify at least two cost components separately, cover a sample of at least 100 organizations, and be published after 2012.\"}"
      }
    ]
  },
